 #:seon.task{:name "a-stale-heartbeat-reads-unknown"
             :plan "Card C3. The heartbeat is a cron task with a function: ping every graph in the registry and its child registries, write `:seon.agent/observed` only when it changed, write `:seon.cluster/heartbeat-t` per period; health reads an observation or heartbeat older than two periods as unknown; the reconciler never reads it. Period a dial `:seon.config.cluster/heartbeat-ms`, never under 10 s. Read `seon.cluster.status/snapshot`, `seon.health/report` and `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/runtime-follows-db.md`."}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-livelocked-agent-reads-as-a-health-observation"
             :plan "S5 run 2 (`docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-14/s5-run-2.md#Broken first`, 2026-10-14 10:57-11:06 UTC): the Design attempt `9107c5cedfe0` (agent `attempt/9107c5cedfe0`) re-entered `seon.turn/prove-turn` on every step for about 3.5 minutes with 0 model calls: its turn thread reached 209 s CPU by 11:04:17, the JVM allocated about 3 GB/s (25.8 GB and 22 s CPU in 8 s; 89 GB and 80 s in 25 s), and open faults stayed 2 (both from before the run). Nothing in `runtime_status`, `seon.health/report` or the faults said so; root found it only by sampling threads. CLAUDE.md says health is a positive observation (a stale observation or silence reads as unknown) and an ordinary operation over one second is a finding. Done when an agent whose turn thread keeps consuming CPU with no recorded progress (no turn, no evaluation, no attempt end, no new test run) reads as not healthy in `seon.health/report` and `seon.cluster.status/snapshot`, naming the agent, its situation from `seon.turn/next-agent-work` and the CPU and allocation it spent, proven as a row in an existing health test that drives a chain agent whose `next-agent-work` repeats the same situation. Derive it from the heartbeat observation `a-stale-heartbeat-reads-unknown` already specifies rather than a second watcher. Related: `seon.turn/a-prove-pass-that-changes-nothing-is-not-proposed-again` removes this cause; this task keeps the next livelock from being silent."}]
